  "body": "Currently, as far as I can tell, having studied the [spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#color-state-(type=color)) and experimented for a while, there's no way for a script to detect the following scenario:\r\n\r\n1. The user clicks on the color well to change a color and a color picker window opens.\r\n2. They fiddle around with the color settings in the color picker.\r\n3. \"input\" events get sent each time a change is made, so the script can, for example, display a preview of what the content might look like in that color.\r\n4. The user decides not to change the color after all and closes the color picker, or clicks a cancel button, without accepting the currently previewed color.\r\n5. The color picker vanishes but the script has no way to know that this has happened. Any preview is left in the last color examined by the user, even though the value of the input has not been changed.\r\n\r\nIt would be helpful if in step 5, an event such as \"cancel\" were sent to the input element so that interested parties would know that the color was not changed after all. This would let the script close its preview, or revert anything that was demonstrating the color back to the original color.",
